Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action contemporary drama; naturalistic faces, worn fabrics, Milwaukee grit and soft Pacific Northwest light; one consistent live-action look for all cast and locations.

FADE IN:

EXT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT BUILDING - DAY

A fifth-floor brick walk-up in downtown Milwaukee. Wind pushes trash along the curb. NICK OLSON, mid-20s, tall and thick, reddish-brown hair uncombed, scars on chin and over left eyebrow, in a worn work jacket, hauls six grocery bags up the stairs like a strongman. PETER OLSON, early 20s, leaner, quieter, same rough family look, shorter hair, carries the rest.

(SOUND: traffic hiss, boots on metal stairs, bags rustling)

PETER (V.O.)
Big brother was mine. Not the book. My real Big Brother. Nick. Face like a puzzle held with glue-gun scars. People moved when he walked in.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Cramped fifth-floor flat. MA OLSON, 60s, tired brown eyes, brown hair, house dress, sits near a glowing TV. Game-show applause leaks from the set. Nick drops bags on the counter. Peter sets his down gentler.

(SOUND: game-show applause, fridge door, distant street noise)

NICK
What’s Ma doin’ back in the bedroom at this time of day? You got to keep an eye on her.

PETER
I will.

NICK
That’s how old people go downhill.

Peter nods. Nick’s burnt-orange 1980 Trans Am keys hit the basket by the door.

EXT. MOXIE’S GYM - DAY

Corner gym. Through the glass: a man mid-preacher-curl with a 40-pound bar sees Nick enter, drops the bar, steps aside. Nick nods, racks a heavier bar.

(SOUND: iron plates clank, gym echo, bar hits rubber)

PETER (V.O.)
He took over the place. Fifty pounds for warm-ups. I ain’t ever curling that. That’s okay. He’s my brother. He’ll stand up for me.

EXT. ALLEY BEHIND KIRK STREET - DAY (FLASH)

Kids’ kickball. Dented garbage-can lids for bases. LITTLE MARK boots a ball straight through a kitchen window. Glass explodes. MRS. ENGEL yells. Young Nick, about twelve, steps forward.

(SOUND: ball thud, glass shatter, woman shouting)

YOUNG NICK
I did it.

PETER (V.O.)
He never told Ma it was Mark. Gave her the sixty bucks a week later. No idea where a twelve-year-old got it. Proud of him.

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

Peter in a stained apron spins dough. Through the front glass, SIONNA, mid-20s, short brown hair, pale skin, light-blue eyes, black V-neck, fish-symbol necklace, jeans, blue tennis shoes, backpack, trips on the sidewalk cement and brushes into him as he steps out.

(SOUND: street traffic, dough slap, her stumble, soft impact)

PETER
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

She brushes her shirt. Their eyes lock. She looks back over her shoulder as she starts to leave.

PETER
I work here. What’s your name?

SIONNA
Sionna. Irish goddess. River Shannon.

PETER
Want pizza?

SIONNA
I don’t eat pizza. Coffee sometime. Call me.

She writes a number. Walks away smooth as a wave. Peter stares.

(SOUND: pen on paper, her footsteps fading)

PETER (V.O.)
Pretty. Pale like an old English story. Like I already knew her. Intense.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - KITCHEN - NIGHT

Pill bottles lined in a neat row. Ma rummages, glassy-eyed, mumbling.

(SOUND: bottles clink, fridge hum, her confused mutter)

MA
I can’t find my pain pills. Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta?

PETER
Ma. We don’t go to church. Pills are right in front of you.

She snaps back, empty eyes clearing a fraction.

MA
Oh. Right. I don’t know what got into me.

Peter’s face tightens.

INT. JOE’S BAR - NIGHT

Packers-Bears on the TV. Smoke, pool, darts. Nick slaps Peter’s back, Jack and Coke in hand.

(SOUND: bar roar, game crowd on TV, cue ball crack, bottles)

NICK
Hey, little brother. What’s shakin’?

PETER
Ma was confused today. Church. Mrs. Canasta. Empty eyes.

NICK
I knew she’d go crazy cooped up. Take her to the store. I don’t have money for a nursing home. Do something before she turns into a demented old fart.

Nick’s look could punch. Peter pays and leaves.

EXT. MILWAUKEE STREET / PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

Peter hurries through cold air, ducks into the old library. Wood floors, chain-hung lights, classic stacks. He fills a temporary card, pulls Wuthering Heights.

(SOUND: wind, door chime, pages, stamp on card)

PETER (V.O.)
Who needed school? Everything free in here. Heathcliff and Catherine. Reminded me of her.

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

College crowd, backpacks, steam. Sionna at a back table in a loose green hooded top. Peter sits. Her watery blue eyes hold him.

(SOUND: espresso machine hiss, low chatter, cup set down)

SIONNA
What are you reading?

PETER
Wuthering Heights. Guy and girl. Grew apart. Still in love.

SIONNA
I know. Ghosts. Doubles. Moors. Wild love that never changes destroys you.

Peter leans in, awake inside.

PETER
Dinner Friday?

SIONNA
Absolutely.

INT. PETER’S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Peter bolts upright, sweating. Dream residue: forest, brown mare, linen sleeves, embroidered red tunic, Sionna’s light-blue eyes, first kiss in a grassy field.

(SOUND: bedsprings, his sharp breath, distant night traffic)

PETER
Hold—

PETER (V.O.)
Sionna’s face. Old clothes. Horses. Finest hour. Make-believe. Had to be.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - DAY

Purple mats. Peter in T-shirt and shorts, shirt tucked after it falls over his face, struggling through downward dog beside calm Sionna. Twenty others breathe. After final savasana he lies still, face soft.

(SOUND: instructor’s soft count, collective breath, mat rustle)

SIONNA
You were really great at that.

PETER
I felt dumb. Then… calm. Wouldn’t care if someone called me a pussy.

SIONNA
Your practice is your practice. That’s why they call it practice.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Nick stomps, face red, fists flexing. Ma sits blank in her chair.

(SOUND: door slam echo, floorboards, his heavy boots)

NICK
Ma missed her doctor’s appointment, you ass. Where the hell were you?

PETER
I forgot. I was with someone. A girl. Dates.

NICK
A girl. Priorities, Bro.

Nick slams out. Peter flinches, then steadies.

INT. DOCTOR’S OFFICE / HOSPITAL CORRIDOR - DAY

DR. CARSON faces Peter. Grey sky in the window.

(SOUND: distant siren, clinic PA, chair creak)

DR. CARSON
Blood pressure dangerously high. Risk of stroke. Emergency room. Admit her.

Later: Ma in a hospital bed, three days. Sionna meets Peter outside; her eyes catch light. He almost says he loves her.

(SOUND: hospital beeps, her soft footsteps, city under hum)

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Blue weekly pill organizer on the table. Nick in boots and suede jacket mocks it. Argument detonates.

(SOUND: TV low, door, slap crack, ear ring)

NICK
Nurse Nancy. Chick’s got you wrapped.

PETER
You haven’t even met her. Give her a break!

NICK
Don’t talk to me like that, you little bastard.

PETER
I’m not a bastard! What got into you? Was Ma on crack when she had you?

Nick’s open hand cracks Peter’s head. Door slams. Peter alone, ear ringing.

PETER (V.O.)
Me and Ma and Sionna. Fine without him.

INT. JOE’S BAR - POOL ROOM - NIGHT

Crowded Saturday. AC/DC under the noise. Nick spots ex LINDSEY with a dark-haired man. Rage builds. Peter steps between.

(SOUND: rock music, pool crack, crowd hush, fists)

PETER
Leave it, Nick. You don’t know this guy.

NICK
Keep out of it. You’re next.

Nick’s fist draws back toward Peter’s face in slow motion. JOE and a BOUNCER rush in. Chaos. Peter runs into the cold night, lungs burning, to Sionna’s door.

INT. SIONNA’S DUPLEX - NIGHT

Peter collapses against her. She holds his face.

(SOUND: his ragged breath, distant traffic, her soft voice)

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you. No one who really cares does that. You’re becoming a bravely compassionate being. Don’t let him stop your purpose.

PETER
What is my purpose?

SIONNA
Find the true self. Honor it.

They sleep on the green suede sofa. Later a dream: Sionna rises from a lake as an angel; giant Nick with a mace shrinks under her force.

EXT. MILWAUKEE STREETS / INTERSECTION - NIGHT

Nick’s Trans Am. Rain. Broadside impact at Werner and Lowe. Metal scream. Silence.

(SOUND: tires, crash thunder, glass, then sirens)

INT. POLICE STATION - DAY

Ancient stone. OFFICER DUPUIS, graying, thin mustache, faces hollow Peter.

(SOUND: station buzz, phone ring, styrofoam cup)

OFFICER DUPUIS
Serious accident. Your brother Nicholas in serious condition. Your mother Olivia passed at the scene. Toxicology pending. Possible alcohol.

Peter’s hands shake on cold water.

INT. HOSPITAL ICU - DAY

Nick unconscious, tubes, chest tube, broken femur, bruised face, wild hair. Peter looks through glass, turns away.

(SOUND: monitors beep, ventilator hiss, soft shoes)

PETER (V.O.)
Nick killed her. He fucking killed my mom.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Empty chair. Afghan. Peter breaks, on the floor, screaming into the quiet.

(SOUND: his raw sobs, nothing else)

PETER
Die you jackass. Die.

Sionna holds him. Later: funeral home choices, modest casket, Catholic cemetery, handful of dirt on maple lid.

(SOUND: dirt thunk on wood, quiet sobs)

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY

Days later. Nick awake, sallow, oxygen, leg huge under sheet. Tears.

(SOUND: TV mute click, IV drip, weak voice)

NICK
I fucked up. Three Jack and Cokes. Forgot. Then drove her to church. Vehicular manslaughter. Mistake.

PETER
Whose fault was it, Nick?

Peter walks out on Nick’s twisted face.

INT. SIONNA’S DUPLEX - NIGHT

Months later. Grey day. Nick forces in, bigger, raging, switchblade from coat.

(SOUND: hard knock, door, his boots, her breath, blade snick)

NICK
You’re the reason. School. Ideas. He should’ve watched Ma. Money. Stay away from my brother. Or this blade.

He pins her to the wall, knife at collarbone. Front door slams open. Peter lunges. Struggle. Blade bites. Red on her shirt. Nick elbows Peter, wipes blade, flees into dark.

(SOUND: struggle, her cry, body slide, running feet)

PETER
Holy shit— Nick—

Peter on the floor, hands stacked on her shoulder wound, blood everywhere, dialing 911 with red fingers.

(SOUND: his panicked breath, siren rising, cat yowl)

PETER
Stay with me. Stay.

EXT. SEATTLE STREET - DAY (YEARS LATER)

Seven years on. Grey blanket sky, mist, hoods. PETER, now early 30s, fuller shoulders, calmer face, walks Belltown. Tony’s Boy’s Pizza under a medical building. He moves like he belongs.

(SOUND: light rain, ferry horn distant, keyboard busker, footsteps)

PETER (V.O.)
Years of pain and growth. Own chain now. Tony’s name on the door. Seven years of hatred for my brother. And missing her.

INT. LOWELL’S / PIKE PLACE - DAY

Peter at a window table, Puget Sound, ferry, cinnamon roll untouched. Memory hits: blood, hallway, her pale face, parents’ eyes.

(SOUND: restaurant clink, market murmur, rain on glass)

PETER (V.O.)
I saved her body. Couldn’t save us. She left. Inner wounds linger. Nick got attempted murder plus the DUI that killed Ma. I hope he never gets out. And still—

INT. SEATTLE YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

Candles, citrus-lavender. Gratefulness class. Peter on a mat among ten others. Eyes open with a decision.

(SOUND: soft chant, mats rolling, rain outside)

PETER (V.O.)
Stop judging. Pizza box on the road is only there when I view it. Forgiveness is the key inside me.

INT. PRISON VISITING ROOM - DAY

Green Bay. Plexiglass. Phones. NICK, older, graying, thinner, bags under sad eyes, orange greens, sits. Peter opposite.

(SOUND: locks, muffled voices, phone pickup, hum of fluorescents)

NICK
Hi, bro. Glad you came. Parole maybe in two.

PETER
I’m here to say this once. You’re an asshole. You killed Ma drunk. You held me down so you could live bigger. You tried to kill the love of my life and drove her away. I won’t see you again.

Nick’s eyes shine. Peter steadies.

PETER (CONT’D)
And I forgive you. It sets me free. Releases your control. I still love you for being my brother. Get better. I own a pizza chain in Seattle. I did it, Nick.

NICK
I’m so sorry. You’re a big man. I’m a small one. President Olson. I’m proud of you. I love you.

Guard signals time. Nick is led away. Peter hangs up, light in his chest.

(SOUND: chair scrape, door buzz, his long exhale)

EXT. WISCONSIN HIGHWAY / AIRPORT - DAY

Peter drives rolling green hills, then walks the terminal lighter than air. Boarding pass scanned.

(SOUND: tires on wet road, airport PA, scanner beep)

PETER (V.O.)
Weight of thirty years gone. Somewhere she’s happy. I send him love and never look back.

He whispers to himself, almost smiling.

PETER
That’s me. President Peter Olson.

(MUSIC: low resolved piano and distant ferry horn)

FADE OUT.

THE END
